WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:03.684
There is no such thing as a perfect crystal.

2
00:00:03.684 --> 00:00:07.586
Defects lower the energy of a crystal and make it more stable, so

3
00:00:07.586 --> 00:00:11.262
every real crystal contains some vacancies and impurities.

4
00:00:11.982 --> 00:00:16.872
And a defect is not necessarily a bad thing. Carbon in iron makes steel.

5
00:00:16.872 --> 00:00:19.601
Copper in nickel makes thermocouple wire.

6
00:00:19.601 --> 00:00:21.989
Chromium in iron resists corrosion.

7
00:00:21.989 --> 00:00:24.604
And grain boundaries strengthen metals.

8
00:00:25.023 --> 00:00:27.074
Defects come in three families.

9
00:00:27.074 --> 00:00:30.314
Point defects, like vacancies and impurity atoms.

10
00:00:30.314 --> 00:00:32.905
Line defects, which are dislocations.

11
00:00:32.905 --> 00:00:35.658
And area defects, like grain boundaries.

12
00:00:39.358 --> 00:00:42.643
A vacancy is a lattice site that is missing an atom.

13
00:00:42.643 --> 00:00:45.038
All crystals contain some vacancies.

14
00:00:45.459 --> 00:00:50.907
A self-interstitial is a host atom crowded into the small void between other atoms.

15
00:00:50.907 --> 00:00:53.086
It distorts the lattice so much that

16
00:00:53.086 --> 00:00:56.778
self-interstitials are far less common than vacancies.

17
00:01:00.478 --> 00:01:02.288
How many vacancies are there?

18
00:01:02.288 --> 00:01:05.304
The equilibrium number follows an Arrhenius equation.

19
00:01:05.725 --> 00:01:09.141
N is the number of lattice sites, Q v the energy to

20
00:01:09.141 --> 00:01:12.743
form a vacancy, and T the absolute temperature.

21
00:01:12.743 --> 00:01:16.469
k is Boltzmann's constant: eight point six two times ten

22
00:01:16.469 --> 00:01:19.947
to the minus five electron volts per atom kelvin.

23
00:01:20.366 --> 00:01:25.817
Because it is exponential, the vacancy fraction rises steeply with temperature.

24
00:01:25.817 --> 00:01:29.451
Take logs, and the natural log of N v over N against one

25
00:01:29.451 --> 00:01:35.447
over T is a straight line, with slope minus Q v over k. That is how we measure Q.

26
00:01:35.867 --> 00:01:41.616
Your lecture's example: the fraction of vacant sites in lead at its melting point, six

27
00:01:41.616 --> 00:01:47.121
hundred kelvin, with Q v equal to zero point five five electron volts per atom.

28
00:01:47.541 --> 00:01:52.004
k T is eight point six two times ten to the minus five, times six

29
00:01:52.004 --> 00:01:56.272
hundred: zero point zero five one seven electron volts.

30
00:01:56.272 --> 00:02:02.158
The exponent is minus zero point five five over that, minus ten point six three.

31
00:02:02.578 --> 00:02:07.826
e to the minus ten point six three is two point four times ten to the minus five.

32
00:02:07.826 --> 00:02:10.868
So even at the melting point, only about two or

33
00:02:10.868 --> 00:02:14.148
three of every hundred thousand sites are empty.

34
00:02:17.948 --> 00:02:21.758
An impurity is an atom of a different element added to the host.

35
00:02:21.758 --> 00:02:24.173
In alloys, we add them on purpose.

36
00:02:24.593 --> 00:02:28.255
Add one atom percent tin to lead, and you get a homogeneous

37
00:02:28.255 --> 00:02:31.859
solid solution with the same crystal structure as lead.

38
00:02:31.859 --> 00:02:36.044
Add twenty five atom percent, and the solubility is exceeded.

39
00:02:36.044 --> 00:02:39.067
You get a two phase, heterogeneous alloy.

40
00:02:39.487 --> 00:02:43.311
In a solid solution, the solvent is the host, present in the

41
00:02:43.311 --> 00:02:46.910
greatest amount, and the solute is the minor element.

42
00:02:46.910 --> 00:02:50.847
The crystal structure is unchanged, and no new phase forms.

43
00:02:51.267 --> 00:02:54.020
Solute atoms sit in two kinds of places.

44
00:02:54.020 --> 00:02:58.589
Substitutional: they replace host atoms, like copper in nickel.

45
00:02:58.589 --> 00:03:01.635
Interstitial: small atoms squeeze into the voids

46
00:03:01.635 --> 00:03:04.622
between host atoms, like carbon in iron.

47
00:03:08.422 --> 00:03:10.984
When is substitutional solubility large?

48
00:03:10.984 --> 00:03:13.000
Hume-Rothery gives four rules.

49
00:03:13.420 --> 00:03:18.530
One, atomic size. The radii should differ by no more than about fifteen percent.

50
00:03:18.951 --> 00:03:22.703
Two, electronegativity. The values must be close.

51
00:03:22.703 --> 00:03:27.071
A large difference forms intermetallic compounds instead of a solution.

52
00:03:27.490 --> 00:03:31.244
Three, crystal structure. Both elements should have the same structure.

53
00:03:31.664 --> 00:03:36.064
Four, valence. All else being equal, a solute with a higher valence

54
00:03:36.064 --> 00:03:39.284
dissolves more readily than one with a lower valence.

55
00:03:39.705 --> 00:03:42.220
Copper and nickel pass all of them.

56
00:03:42.220 --> 00:03:46.711
Both are F C C, their radii are zero point one two eight and zero point

57
00:03:46.711 --> 00:03:51.262
one two five nanometers, and their electronegativities are close.

58
00:03:51.262 --> 00:03:54.017
They dissolve in each other completely.

59
00:03:57.817 --> 00:04:00.093
Composition is written two ways.

60
00:04:00.093 --> 00:04:04.062
Weight percent is the mass of an element over the total mass.

61
00:04:04.062 --> 00:04:07.797
Atom percent is its number of moles over the total moles.

62
00:04:08.217 --> 00:04:12.949
Industry uses weight percent, because you weigh what you add to the melt.

63
00:04:12.949 --> 00:04:17.061
To predict defects and properties, we often need atom percent.

64
00:04:17.061 --> 00:04:20.892
The bridge is the atomic weight: moles equal mass over A.

65
00:04:21.312 --> 00:04:26.268
Your lecture's example: zero point eight weight percent carbon steel.

66
00:04:26.268 --> 00:04:28.560
Take a one hundred gram basis.

67
00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:31.781
Carbon: zero point eight grams over twelve point

68
00:04:31.781 --> 00:04:35.064
zero one is zero point zero six six six moles.

69
00:04:35.064 --> 00:04:38.719
Iron: ninety nine point two grams over fifty five point

70
00:04:38.719 --> 00:04:42.002
eight five is one point seven seven six moles.

71
00:04:42.421 --> 00:04:45.322
Atom percent carbon is zero point zero six six six

72
00:04:45.322 --> 00:04:48.456
over the total, one point eight four three.

73
00:04:48.456 --> 00:04:50.893
About three point six atom percent.

74
00:04:50.893 --> 00:04:55.129
So roughly three or four of every hundred atoms are carbon, mostly in

75
00:04:55.129 --> 00:05:00.062
interstitial sites, where they distort the lattice and block dislocations.

76
00:05:03.863 --> 00:05:05.897
Line defects are dislocations.

77
00:05:05.897 --> 00:05:09.194
They come from solidification, from mechanical work like

78
00:05:09.194 --> 00:05:12.382
rolling and drawing, and from thermal stresses.

79
00:05:12.382 --> 00:05:15.625
It is very difficult to make a crystal without them.

80
00:05:16.045 --> 00:05:20.796
An edge dislocation is an extra half plane of atoms inserted into the crystal.

81
00:05:20.796 --> 00:05:25.938
The planes above it are squeezed together, and the planes below are pulled apart.

82
00:05:26.358 --> 00:05:31.749
The Burgers vector, b, gives the size and direction of the lattice distortion.

83
00:05:31.749 --> 00:05:36.329
For an edge dislocation, b is perpendicular to the dislocation line.

84
00:05:36.749 --> 00:05:40.130
Dislocations make metals weaker than they should be, but

85
00:05:40.130 --> 00:05:43.342
they are also what lets metals deform plastically.

86
00:05:43.342 --> 00:05:47.118
Watch: under shear, the dislocation glides through, and

87
00:05:47.118 --> 00:05:50.443
the top of the crystal shifts by one Burgers vector.

88
00:05:53.362 --> 00:05:58.269
A screw dislocation is like cutting the crystal halfway and sliding one side.

89
00:05:58.269 --> 00:06:03.352
The atomic planes follow a helical path, and here b is parallel to the line.

90
00:06:03.772 --> 00:06:06.581
Most real dislocations are mixed: pure screw at one

91
00:06:06.581 --> 00:06:09.901
end, pure edge at the other, and mixed in between.

92
00:06:13.801 --> 00:06:15.718
Here is the summary of topic five.

93
00:06:16.138 --> 00:06:20.718
Vacancies always exist, and their number follows the Arrhenius equation.

94
00:06:21.138 --> 00:06:23.634
Solutes go in substitutional or interstitial

95
00:06:23.634 --> 00:06:26.528
sites, until the solubility is exceeded.

96
00:06:26.948 --> 00:06:31.533
Hume-Rothery: size, electronegativity, crystal structure and valence.

97
00:06:31.953 --> 00:06:35.484
Convert weight to atom percent through the atomic weights.

98
00:06:35.484 --> 00:06:38.634
And dislocations come in edge, screw and mixed.

99
00:06:38.634 --> 00:06:42.925
Next, the defects that live between grains, and how we see them.
